  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe front is finally off the Texas coast leaving partly cloudy and \nmostly dry conditions in its wake. Some isolated residual showers \nwill be possible overnight into the morning. Monday will be chilly \nwith highs only topping out the mid 60s. This will be the opening \nact of a fairly chilly week. We will see a secondary front push \nthrough on Tuesday, ensuring a chilly Thanksgiving across the \narea. Isolated to scattered showers will be possible with maybe a \nthunderstorm or two thanks to some weak isentropic lift in the \nregion and an associated shortwave. This front will give us a \nreinforcing surge of cooler air as high pressure builds in behind \nit. High temperatures will be in the upper 50s to low 60s through \nthe remainder of the week! Lows on Tuesday night and Wednesday \nnight will be the coldest and there is a possibility that we could\nsee temperatures flirt near freezing. Will be something else to \nkeep in mind as we progress through this week.\n\nMinor coastal flooding will remain a possibility into the beginning \nof the week. Long period swells remain a threat with tides running \naround 2.0ft MSL at Bob Hall Pier. Will have to monitor through the \nday to see if another coastal flood advisory may be warranted.",
